{
  "consensus": [
    "Goodman-Bacon (2021) decomposition showing TWFE equals a weighted average of all possible 2x2 DiD comparisons is unanimously accepted; mid-panel adopters receive largest variance-driven weights, and 'forbidden comparisons' (later-treated units using earlier-treated as controls) emerge under staggered adoption.",
    "All three estimator families (Callaway-Sant'Anna, Sun-Abraham, Borusyak-Jaravel-Spiess) eliminate forbidden comparisons by construction and accommodate unrestricted treatment-effect heterogeneity; treatment-effect homogeneity is no longer a substantive assumption.",
    "Callaway-Sant'Anna is identified as the modal default/off-the-shelf heterogeneity-robust estimator, with Sun-Abraham commonly used as a companion for event-study specifications and Borusyak-Jaravel-Spiess emerging as the efficiency-oriented choice.",
    "No-anticipation is a maintained assumption across all three estimators, though each accommodates anticipation via parameter choices (CS: anticipation horizon; SA/BJS: dropping leads, model restrictions).",
    "CS uniquely supports conditional parallel trends via covariates using OR/IPW/doubly-robust estimation; SA and BJS require unconditional parallel trends across the relevant comparison units.",
    "CS uses only the last pre-treatment period (g-1) as the base period for each cohort; BJS/global imputation uses all pre-treatment periods, which is the source of its efficiency advantage.",
    "Roth (2022) critique identifies two problems: (1) low power of conventional pre-trend tests and (2) pre-test bias from conditioning on passing the test; his calibration finds both issues consequential in published AER/QJE/JPE work.",
    "Sun-Abraham's core contribution — that apparent pre-trends can arise solely from treatment-effect heterogeneity — directly motivates the new estimators and partially addresses Roth's contamination concern.",
    "BJS's separation of estimation (using only untreated observations to fit unit/time FE) from testing (comparing imputed vs. actual pre-treatment outcomes) is widely identified as the most direct architectural answer to Roth's pre-test bias concern.",
    "Rambachan-Roth (2023) HonestDiD sensitivity analysis (with M-bar or smoothness restrictions) is the recommended complement to the new estimators for addressing potential parallel-trends violations.",
    "Applied authors rarely justify estimator choice via their own Monte Carlo simulations; justification typically consists of citing methodological references plus reporting multiple estimators or sensitivity analyses.",
    "BJS imputation estimator produces substantially shorter confidence intervals (approximately 50% shorter than CS, 2-3.5x shorter than SA) under plausible homogeneity, but loses this efficiency edge under heterogeneity."
